    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<h2>What is a solar street light with CCTV camera?</h2>
<p>A <strong>solar street light with CCTV camera</strong> combines four things on one pole: an LED light head, a security camera (usually a pan-tilt-zoom or dual-lens unit), a solar panel and a battery. Some designs put everything in one housing, the classic <strong>all in one solar street light with camera</strong>. Others are "split" designs, with a separate panel on a bracket above the light and the battery in a box or inside the pole.</p>
<p>The camera connects over Wi-Fi or 4G LTE, records to a microSD card and sends alerts to a phone app. The light usually runs on a dusk-to-dawn schedule with a motion-triggered brightness boost. The idea is attractive: one install, no trenching, light and surveillance anywhere the sun shines.</p>
<p>The reality depends heavily on what you buy. A well-specified unit performs well. Many low-cost listings overstate their specs so much that the light dims after a few hours and the camera drops offline on cloudy days. This guide explains how to tell the difference before you pay.</p>

<h2>Solar street light with CCTV camera price ranges</h2>
<p>The prices below are typical US retail and trade ranges at time of writing (late 2026). They are not quotes and vary by supplier, volume, shipping and spec.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Typical unit price</th><th>Typical specs</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Consumer all-in-one (Wi-Fi)</td><td>~$80–200</td><td>10–40 W panel, small LED head, 2–4 MP PTZ camera, microSD</td><td>Driveways, yards, barns with Wi-Fi coverage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Consumer all-in-one (4G)</td><td>~$120–300</td><td>As above with LTE modem; needs a SIM and data plan</td><td>Gates, farms, sites with no Wi-Fi</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prosumer split design</td><td>~$300–800</td><td>50–120 W panel, larger LiFePO4 battery, better camera</td><td>Long driveways, small parking areas, rural lanes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Commercial integrated pole</td><td>~$500–3,000+</td><td>Engineered panel and battery for several days of autonomy, rated optics, managed camera</td><td>Parking lots, campuses, private roads, municipalities</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pole (steel or aluminum)</td><td>~$100–1,500+</td><td>3–10 m depending on height and wind rating</td><td>Usually sold separately</td></tr>
<tr><td>Installation</td><td>~$200–1,500+ per pole</td><td>Foundation, pole setting, equipment lift</td><td>Commercial or tall poles</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>For a single driveway light on an existing post you might spend $100–250 all in. A properly engineered commercial pole with foundation and installation often lands at $2,000–5,000 or more per location. The <strong>price of solar light street</strong> units scales mostly with panel size and battery capacity, which are exactly the parts that decide whether the system works in winter.</p>
<div class="keyfacts"><div><b>$80–300</b><span>typical consumer all-in-one unit</span></div><div><b>$500–3,000+</b><span>typical commercial integrated pole</span></div><div><b>3–10 m</b><span>common pole heights</span></div></div>

<h2>What drives the solar street light with CCTV camera price?</h2>
<p>Two units that look identical in photos can differ in price by a factor of five. The difference is almost always in the parts you cannot see in a product shot.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Panel size.</strong> The biggest single cost driver. More watts means more energy, which means a brighter light, a camera that stays online longer and better winter performance.</li>
<li><strong>Battery capacity and chemistry.</strong> LiFePO4 batteries cost more than standard lithium-ion but tolerate heat, deep cycling and years of daily use better. Larger batteries add nights of autonomy.</li>
<li><strong>Camera quality and connectivity.</strong> A 4G modem adds cost over Wi-Fi, and better sensors, optical zoom and on-device person detection add more.</li>
<li><strong>Optics and LED quality.</strong> Commercial lights use lenses that shape the beam along a road or across a lot instead of throwing light everywhere.</li>
<li><strong>Housing, wind rating and warranty.</strong> Commercial poles are engineered for wind loads and come with multi-year warranties; budget units rarely state either.</li>
</ul>
<p>In practice, the solar street light with CCTV camera price you pay buys energy capacity more than features. A cheaper unit with an honest 40 W panel often outperforms a pricier one with a flashy camera and a 15 W panel.</p>

<h2>Why the specs on cheap units do not add up</h2>
<p>Low-cost listings often advertise "1000 W", "2000 W" or "50,000 lumens" on a light with a panel the size of a laptop. Those numbers describe a theoretical maximum, an equivalent to an old halogen lamp, or nothing real at all. The light cannot sustain that output, because the panel and battery cannot supply the energy.</p>
<p>You can check any listing with simple arithmetic. You only need two figures that are hard to fake: the <strong>panel’s physical size</strong> (or honest wattage) and the <strong>battery capacity in Wh</strong>.</p>
<h3>Step 1: estimate daily energy from the panel</h3>
<p>A solar panel produces roughly its rated watts multiplied by peak sun hours (PSH), times a real-world efficiency factor. PSH is typically 4–6 in summer or in the sunbelt and 2–3 in a northern US winter. Real systems deliver around 60–80 % of the panel rating after angle, heat, dirt and charging losses.</p>
<blockquote>Daily energy (Wh) ≈ panel watts × peak sun hours × 0.7</blockquote>
<p>A 20 W panel in a 3-PSH winter: 20 × 3 × 0.7 ≈ 42 Wh per day. In a 5-PSH summer: about 70 Wh per day.</p>
<h3>Step 2: check the panel size against its claimed watts</h3>
<p>Typical monocrystalline panels produce around 180–220 W per square meter of panel area in full sun. A panel measuring 50 × 35 cm (0.175 m²) is therefore roughly a 30–40 W panel, no matter what the listing says. If the claimed wattage is wildly higher than the size allows, treat every other spec on the listing with suspicion.</p>
<h3>Step 3: compare with the light’s needs</h3>
<p>Good LEDs produce around 130–180 lumens per watt; budget units are often lower. To light all night (say 12 hours in winter) at an average of 10 W, the light alone needs 120 Wh. A 42 Wh daily harvest cannot do that, which is why cheap units run at a dim level most of the night and only brighten on motion. That is a reasonable design for a driveway, but it is not "50,000 lumens".</p>
<h3>Step 4: add the camera</h3>
<p>This is the part most listings skip. A continuously powered Wi-Fi or 4G PTZ camera commonly draws around 3–6 W, so running it 24 hours uses roughly 70–145 Wh a day, often more than the light. Small all-in-one units handle this by putting the camera in a low-power sleep mode that wakes on motion, which works, but means no continuous recording and slower wake-up.</p>
<h3>Step 5: check the battery reserve</h3>
<p>Battery capacity in Wh is mAh × voltage ÷ 1,000. A "30,000 mAh" LiFePO4 battery at 3.2 V is about 96 Wh. If the system uses 60 Wh a night, that battery covers about one and a half nights with no sun, before accounting for cold weather and the fact that batteries should not be run completely flat. Commercial systems are usually designed for three to five days of autonomy.</p>
<div class="callout tip"><strong>Quick sanity check</strong><p>Divide the battery Wh by your expected nightly use (light plus camera). If the answer is less than 2, expect the system to struggle after a single cloudy day. Our <a href="/tools/solar-camera-battery-calculator/">solar camera battery calculator</a> runs the camera side of this math for your location.</p></div>

<h2>Solar powered street light with camera: what a realistic spec looks like</h2>
<p>To light a driveway at a modest level all night and keep a motion-triggered camera online year-round in a mid-latitude US location, a reasonable spec looks roughly like this. Adjust upward for northern winters, heavy camera use or brighter light.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Driveway / yard</th><th>Parking lot / private road</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Panel</td><td>30–60 W</td><td>80–200 W</td></tr>
<tr><td>Battery</td><td>100–250 Wh (LiFePO4 preferred)</td><td>400–1,200 Wh</td></tr>
<tr><td>Light output</td><td>1,000–3,000 real lumens, dimmed most of the night</td><td>4,000–10,000+ real lumens with rated optics</td></tr>
<tr><td>Camera</td><td>2–4 MP, motion-triggered, microSD</td><td>4 MP+, continuous or scheduled recording, remote management</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mounting height</td><td>3–5 m</td><td>6–10 m</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>If a listing pairs a 15 W panel with a promise of all-night full brightness and a 24/7 camera, the numbers do not work, regardless of price.</p>

<h2>Where solar streetlights with security cameras make sense</h2>
<figure><img src="/assets/img/street-light-cctv-2.webp" alt="Solar street light with security camera lighting a rural lane at dusk" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Rural lanes, gates and parking areas far from power are where integrated solar light-and-camera poles earn their keep.</figcaption></figure>
<ul>
<li><strong>Long driveways and gates.</strong> Light and a camera where a car stops, without trenching from the house. For gate-specific framing, see our <a href="/guides/solar-gate-camera/">solar gate camera guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Farm yards and barns.</strong> A pole in the equipment yard lights the area and records activity. Our <a href="/use-cases/solar-cameras-for-farms/">solar cameras for farms</a> guide covers zone planning.</li>
<li><strong>Small parking lots.</strong> Churches, clubs, trailheads and small businesses where running power is expensive.</li>
<li><strong>Rural roads and private lanes.</strong> Where a community or landowner wants light and visibility at a junction or entrance.</li>
<li><strong>Construction and temporary sites.</strong> Some contractors use them alongside other measures; see <a href="/use-cases/solar-cameras-for-construction-sites/">solar cameras for construction sites</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>They make less sense where mains power is already close (a wired light and camera are cheaper to run and more reliable), in heavily shaded spots, or where you need high-quality continuous recording from several angles.</p>

<h2>Pole height and spacing</h2>
<p>Height affects both light spread and camera usefulness. A higher light spreads further but less intensely; a higher camera sees more but identifies less.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Typical pole height</th><th>Typical spacing</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Garden paths, walkways</td><td>3–4 m</td><td>10–15 m</td></tr>
<tr><td>Driveways</td><td>4–6 m</td><td>15–25 m</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parking lots</td><td>6–10 m</td><td>20–30 m</td></tr>
<tr><td>Private roads</td><td>6–9 m</td><td>25–40 m, staggered</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>A common rule of thumb places poles about 3–4 times their mounting height apart for continuous lighting, closer if you want even illumination. For cameras, identifying a face generally needs the subject within roughly 5–10 m on a typical 2–4 MP lens, so a camera at the top of a 9 m pole is better for overview than identification. The <a href="/tools/camera-coverage-calculator/">camera coverage calculator</a> shows what a given lens can see at a distance, and the <a href="/tools/cctv-camera-planner/">CCTV camera planner</a> helps count cameras.</p>
<p>Point the panel true south (in the northern hemisphere) at a tilt near your latitude, steeper for winter-optimized systems. Many all-in-one units have a fixed panel angle; split designs let you adjust it. The <a href="/tools/solar-panel-angle-calculator/">solar panel angle calculator</a> gives the best angle for your location, and <a href="https://pvwatts.nrel.gov/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">NREL PVWatts</a> estimates monthly solar output if you want detailed numbers.</p>

<h2>Permits for public roads</h2>
<p>On your own driveway or farm, you can usually install a solar light pole without a permit, though tall poles may fall under local zoning or height rules. Public roads are different. Street lighting in the public right-of-way is typically controlled by the city, county or state transportation department, and must meet lighting standards, structural and wind-load requirements, and sometimes dark-sky ordinances that limit upward light. Cameras on public roads raise additional privacy and records questions.</p>
<p>If you are a homeowners association, business or community group considering lights along a public road, contact the local public works or transportation department before buying anything. For private roads, check HOA covenants and any county requirements.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Privacy</strong><p>Aim cameras at your own property, entrance or lot. Avoid recording into neighbors’ homes, and leave audio off unless you know your state’s consent rules, which are stricter for audio than for video.</p></div>

<h2>Integrated pole vs separate light and camera</h2>
<p>You can buy a combined unit, or put a solar floodlight and a separate solar camera on the same pole. Each approach has a place.</p>
<div class="proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Integrated (all-in-one)</h4><ul><li>One product, one install</li><li>Clean look, one app</li><li>Often cheaper at the low end</li><li>Commercial versions engineered as a system</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Separate light + camera</h4><ul><li>Light and camera do not share a battery, so a dim night does not kill recording</li><li>Replace or upgrade one without the other</li><li>Choose a better camera than integrated units offer</li><li>Each part can be aimed and positioned independently</li></ul></div></div>
<p>For a single driveway or yard, a separate solar floodlight and a good solar camera on the same post is often the more reliable option at a similar price. For a parking lot or private road with several poles, a properly engineered integrated system simplifies management. Read our <a href="/best-solar-security-cameras/">best solar security cameras</a> guide if you go the separate route, and <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-work-at-night/">do solar cameras work at night</a> for how lighting improves camera footage.</p>

<h2>Buying checklist</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Find the real panel wattage</strong><p>Check the panel dimensions. Roughly 180–220 W per square meter is the ceiling for typical panels.</p></li>
<li><strong>Find battery capacity in Wh</strong><p>Convert mAh × volts ÷ 1,000. LiFePO4 handles heat and cycling better than standard lithium-ion.</p></li>
<li><strong>Ask for real lumens and LED wattage</strong><p>Not "equivalent" watts. Check whether output dims through the night.</p></li>
<li><strong>Confirm camera connectivity and storage</strong><p>Wi-Fi range at the pole, or 4G signal and data plan. microSD for local recording. Estimate data with the <a href="/tools/4g-data-and-storage-calculator/">4G data calculator</a>.</p></li>
<li><strong>Check autonomy and warranty</strong><p>How many nights without sun the system is designed for, and how long the battery is warranted.</p></li>
<li><strong>Plan the pole and permits</strong><p>Height, foundation, wind rating, and any permits for public roads.</p></li>
</ol>
